## 2. Choose a DEM source that gives you raw values  

| Source | How to read the values | Typical URL pattern |
|--------|-----------------------|---------------------|
| **Mapbox Terrain-RGB** (or any “terrain-rgb” tileset) | Decode the 24-bit RGB → elevation (meters) using the formula `e = (R * 256 * 256 + G * 256 + B) / 10 - 10000` | `https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.pngraw?access_token=…` |
| **Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF (COG)** | Use `geotiff.js` (or `georaster`) to read the raster band directly | `https://myserver/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.cog.tif` |
| **Custom PNG/WEBP DEM** | Same as Terrain-RGB if you encode elevation yourself | any tile URL that returns an image you control |

> **Tip:** If you already have a `raster-dem` source in your style, you can reuse its URL template – just fetch the tile yourself with `fetch()` and decode it.

---

## 3. Turn a DEM tile into a numeric grid  

Below is a minimal example using **Terrain-RGB** and **geotiff.js** (the same approach works for a COG).

```js
import GeoTIFF from 'geotiff';
import {contour} from 'd3-contour';   // Marching Squares implementation
import {FeatureCollection, LineString} from '@turf/helpers';

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Fetch & decode a single DEM tile (z/x/y)
// ---------------------------------------------------
async function getElevationGrid(z, x, y) {
  const url = `https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/${z}/${x}/${y}.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN`;
  const response = await fetch(url);
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // Decode PNG → Uint8Array (R,G,B per pixel)
  const img = await createImageBitmap(new Blob([arrayBuffer]));
  const canvas = new OffscreenCanvas(img.width, img.height);
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  const {data} = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, img.width, img.height);

  const width = img.width, height = img.height;
  const grid = new Float32Array(width * height);

  // Convert RGB → elevation (meters)
  for (let i = 0, j = 0; i < data.length; i += 4, j++) {
    const R = data[i];
    const G = data[i + 1];
    const B = data[i + 2];
    const elevation = (R * 256 * 256 + G * 256 + B) / 10 - 10000;
    grid[j] = elevation;
  }

  return {grid, width, height};
}
```

> **Why an OffscreenCanvas?**  
> It runs in a Web Worker (no UI thread) and works in all modern browsers. If you stay on the main thread you can use a normal `<canvas>`.

---

## 4. Generate contours with **d3-contour** (or any Marching-Squares lib)

`d3-contour` expects a **flat array** of values and the grid dimensions. It returns GeoJSON-compatible `MultiLineString`s for each threshold you ask for.

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Run the contour algorithm
// ---------------------------------------------------
function makeContours({grid, width, height}, interval = 100, min = 0, max = 3000) {
  const thresholds = [];
  for (let v = min; v <= max; v += interval) thresholds.push(v);

  // d3-contour works in pixel space (0..width, 0..height)
  const contours = contour()
    .size([width, height])
    .thresholds(thresholds)
    (grid);

  // Convert each contour to GeoJSON in EPSG:3857 (Web Mercator)
  const features = contours.map(c => ({
    type: 'Feature',
    properties: {elevation: c.value},
    geometry: c.geometry   // already a MultiLineString
  }));

  return {
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features
  };
}
```

### 4.1. Re-project pixel coordinates to **Lng/Lat**

The contour geometry is still in **pixel coordinates** relative to the tile. We need to map each `(x, y)` back to geographic coordinates.

```js
function pixelToLngLat(x, y, z, tileX, tileY) {
  const n = Math.pow(2, z);
  const lon = (tileX + x / 256) / n * 360 - 180;
  const latRad = Math.atan(Math.sinh(Math.PI * (1 - 2 * (tileY + y / 256) / n)));
  const lat = latRad * 180 / Math.PI;
  return [lon, lat];
}

function reprojectFeatureCollection(fc, z, tileX, tileY) {
  fc.features = fc.features.map(f => {
    const geom = f.geometry;
    // MultiLineString: [[[x,y],...], ...]
    geom.coordinates = geom.coordinates.map(ring =>
      ring.map(([px, py]) => pixelToLngLat(px, py, z, tileX, tileY))
    );
    return f;
  });
  return fc;
}
```

Now you have a **GeoJSON FeatureCollection** of contour lines in WGS-84 that can be fed straight into MapLibre.

---

## 5. Put it together in a **Web Worker**  

Running the above on the UI thread will freeze the map. A tiny worker does the job:

```js
// worker.js
self.onmessage = async e => {
  const {z, x, y, interval, min, max} = e.data;
  const dem = await getElevationGrid(z, x, y);
  let fc = makeContours(dem, interval, min, max);
  fc = reprojectFeatureCollection(fc, z, x, y);
  self.postMessage({z, x, y, geojson: fc});
};
```

In the main thread:

```js
const worker = new Worker('worker.js');

function requestContours(z, x, y) {
  worker.postMessage({z, x, y, interval: 100, min: 0, max: 3000});
}

// Listen for results
worker.onmessage = e => {
  const {z, x, y, geojson} = e.data;
  const sourceId = `contours-${z}-${x}-${y}`;

  // Add / replace source
  if (map.getSource(sourceId)) {
    map.getSource(sourceId).setData(geojson);
  } else {
    map.addSource(sourceId, {type: 'geojson', data: geojson});
    map.addLayer({
      id: `${sourceId}-line`,
      type: 'line',
      source: sourceId,
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#000',
        'line-width': 1,
        'line-opacity': 0.6
      }
    });
  }
};
```

### When to request?

* **On `moveend`** – iterate over all tiles that are currently visible at the current zoom (or a lower zoom for performance) and request contours for each.
* **Cache** the GeoJSON per tile (`z/x/y`) in `IndexedDB` or an in-memory `Map` so you never recompute the same tile twice.
* **Simplify** the lines (e.g., `turf.simplify`) before adding them if you need to keep the payload small.

