## TL;DR  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on the fly** from a raster-DEM source in a MapLibre GL JS map, but you have to do the heavy lifting yourself (or in a tiny server-less helper). The typical recipe is:

1. **Pull the raw elevation values** from the DEM tiles (e.g. Mapbox Terrain-RGB or a GeoTIFF tile service).  
2. **Run a contouring algorithm** (Marching Squares / Marching Cubes) on that grid in a Web Worker.  
3. **Emit GeoJSON LineStrings** (or a `FeatureCollection`) and add them to the map as a `geojson` source + a `line` layer.  
4. **Cache & update** the contours as the user pans/zooms, respecting the current zoom-level (coarser contours at low zoom, finer at high zoom).

Below is a step-by-step guide, a working code sketch, and a list of tools & gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1. Why MapLibre can’t do it for you out-of-the-box  

MapLibre GL JS knows how to:

* render raster-DEM tiles for hill-shade or terrain-exaggeration (`raster-dem` source)  
* request vector tiles that already contain contour lines (if you pre-generate them)

But it **does not** expose the raw elevation values to the style engine, nor does it have a built-in “contour” renderer. So you have to:

* **Read the DEM data yourself** (decode the RGB → elevation or read a GeoTIFF)  
* **Run a contour algorithm** in JavaScript (or a tiny serverless function)  
* **Feed the result back to MapLibre** as a normal `geojson` source.

---

## 2. High-level workflow

```
+-------------------+        +-------------------+        +-------------------+
|  MapLibre view    |  <---> |  DEM tile fetch   |  <---> |  DEM decoder      |
|  (raster-dem)     |        |  (png/geoTIFF)    |        |  (geotiff.js)     |
+-------------------+        +-------------------+        +-------------------+
        ^                               |                         |
        |                               v                         v
        |                     +-------------------+   +-------------------+
        |                     |  Elevation grid   |   |  Contour worker   |
        |                     +-------------------+   +-------------------+
        |                               |                         |
        |                               v                         |
        |                     +-------------------+   +-------------------+
        |                     |  d3-contour /     |   |  GeoJSON output   |
        |                     |  marching-squares|   +-------------------+
        |                     +-------------------+            |
        |                               |                     v
        |                               v            +-------------------+
        |                     +-------------------+ |  MapLibre add    |
        |                     |  GeoJSON Feature  | |  source + layer  |
        |                     +-------------------+ +-------------------+
        +---------------------------------------------------------------+
```

* **Step 1 – Tile fetch** – Use the same URL pattern you already use for the `raster-dem` source (e.g. `https://example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png`).  
* **Step 2 – Decode** – Convert the RGB values to meters (or read a GeoTIFF).  
* **Step 3 – Grid** – Build a 2-D array of elevation values for the tile (or a stitched block of adjacent tiles).  
* **Step 4 – Contour** – Run a contouring algorithm at the desired interval(s).  
* **Step 5 – GeoJSON** – Turn each contour line into a `LineString` Feature, re-project the pixel coordinates to WGS-84 (or Web Mercator).  
* **Step 6 – Render** – Add the GeoJSON as a source and style it with a `line` layer.

---

## 3. Picking the right tools

| Task | Recommended library / API | Why |
|------|---------------------------|-----|
| **Fetch raster tiles** | `fetch()` + `Response.arrayBuffer()` | Simple, works in browsers. |
| **Decode Terrain-RGB** | **No library needed** – just bit-shifts (`(R*256*256 + G*256 + B) / 10`) | Terrain-RGB spec is trivial. |
| **Decode GeoTIFF** | **geotiff.js** (v2+) | Reads arbitrary GeoTIFFs, gives you a Float32Array of elevations. |
| **Contour generation** | **d3-contour** (Marching Squares) or **marchingsquares.js** | Fast, works on a regular grid, returns GeoJSON-compatible contours. |
| **Heavy lifting off-main-thread** | **Web Worker** (or **Comlink** wrapper) | Keeps UI responsive. |
| **Simplify / smooth** | **simplify-geojson** or **turf.simplify** | Reduces vertex count for high-zoom tiles. |
| **Cache** | IndexedDB (`idb-keyval`) or in-memory `Map` | Avoid re-computing the same tile. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a server that can run GDAL, you could expose a tiny “contour-as-GeoJSON” endpoint (`/contour/{z}/{x}/{y}?interval=10`). That offloads the CPU from the client and works even on low-powered phones. The client-only approach below works completely offline.

---

## 4. Detailed implementation (client-only)

Below is a **complete, minimal example** that you can copy-paste into a page that already loads MapLibre GL JS (v2.x). It assumes you are using the **Mapbox Terrain-RGB** tiles (or any other 256-pixel PNG tiles that follow the same encoding).

### 4.1. HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre Contours on the Fly</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/d3-contour@3"></script>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script src="contour-worker.js" type="module"></script>
<script src="main.js" type="module"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

* `contour-worker.js` – the Web Worker that does the heavy lifting.  
* `main.js` – the MapLibre glue code.

### 4.2. The Web Worker (`contour-worker.js`)

```js
// contour-worker.js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// This worker receives a message:
//   { tileKey, elevationGrid, width, height, intervals }
// and returns GeoJSON contours for those intervals.
// ---------------------------------------------------
importScripts('https://unpkg.com/d3-contour@3/dist/d3-contour.min.js');

self.onmessage = async (e) => {
  const { tileKey, elevationGrid, width, height, intervals, bbox } = e.data;
  // d3-contour expects a flat Float32Array (row-major order)
  const contours = [];

  intervals.forEach((interval) => {
    const iso = d3.contours()
      .size([width, height])
      .thresholds([interval])
      (elevationGrid)[0]; // only one threshold → first element

    // iso.coordinates is an array of rings (each ring = array of [x,y] pixel coords)
    iso.coordinates.forEach((ring) => {
      // Convert pixel → lng/lat using the tile bbox (Web Mercator)
      const line = ring.map(([px, py]) => {
        const x = bbox[0] + (px / width) * (bbox[2] - bbox[0]); // lng
        const y = bbox[3] - (py / height) * (bbox[3] - bbox[1]); // lat (note Y flip)
        return [x, y];
      });

      contours.push({
        type: 'Feature',
        properties: { interval },
        geometry: {
          type: 'LineString',
          coordinates: line,
        },
      });
    });
  });

  self.postMessage({ tileKey, geojson: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: contours } });
};
```

**Explanation of the worker**

* **Input**  
  * `elevationGrid` – a `Float32Array` of size `width * height`.  
  * `bbox` – `[west, south, east, north]` of the tile in **WGS-84** (Web Mercator lat/lng).  
* **Processing**  
  * `d3.contours()` runs Marching Squares for each requested interval.  
  * The resulting polygon rings are turned into `LineString`s.  
* **Output** – a `FeatureCollection` that can be fed straight into a MapLibre `geojson` source.

> **Performance note:** `d3-contour` is fast for a single 256×256 tile (≈ 5 ms). If you stitch 2×2 tiles together you’ll still be under 30 ms on a modern desktop. Use a worker to keep the UI smooth.

